          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A radiologist identifies a mass at the level of the transpyloric plane. Which of the following structures would NOT be found at this level?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pylorus of the stomach</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The pylorus is one of the defining structures of the transpyloric plane — the plane is named after it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hilum of the left kidney</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both renal hila lie at the transpyloric plane (vertebral level L1).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bifurcation of the abdominal aorta</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The aortic bifurcation is at L4, well below the transpyloric plane at L1. The transpyloric plane passes through the pylorus, the pancreatic neck, both renal hila, and the origin of the superior mesenteric artery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neck of the pancreas</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The neck of the pancreas is crossed by the transpyloric plane at L1.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The transpyloric plane at L1 passes through the pylorus, the pancreatic neck, both renal hila, and the origin of the superior mesenteric artery. The aortic bifurcation is at L4, well below this plane."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The aortic bifurcation lies at L4, far below the transpyloric plane. No other single plane carries as many landmarks as the transpyloric plane at L1 — pylorus, pancreatic neck, both renal hila, and the SMA origin."
                }
              ],
