          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A surgeon identifies an inguinal hernia that has entered through the deep inguin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A surgeon identifies an inguinal hernia that has entered through the deep inguinal ring and traverses the full length of the inguinal canal. What is the relationship of this hernia to the inferior epigastric vessels?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Medial to the inferior epigastric vessels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A hernia medial to the inferior epigastric vessels is a direct inguinal hernia, which pushes through Hesselbach's triangle without entering the deep ring.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. An indirect hernia enters through the deep ring, which lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. Remember the mnemonic MALL — Medial = Direct, Lateral = Indirect.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Posterior to the inferior epigastric vessels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Inguinal hernias are classified by their medial or lateral relationship to the inferior epigastric vessels, not by an anterior–posterior relationship.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Anterior to the inferior epigastric vessels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The inferior epigastric vessels define the medial–lateral classification of inguinal hernias. Anterior–posterior position is not used in this classification.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "An indirect hernia enters through the deep inguinal ring, which lies lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. A direct hernia pushes through Hesselbach's triangle, medial to these vessels. The mnemonic MALL helps: Medial = Direct, Lateral = Indirect."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! An indirect hernia enters through the deep ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels, and traverses the full inguinal canal. It carries a higher risk of strangulation because the tight deep ring can compress the hernial contents."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
