          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A surgeon performs an appendicectomy through a gridiron incision at McBurney's p</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A surgeon performs an appendicectomy through a gridiron incision at McBurney's point. What is the key advantage of this incision over a simple transverse cut through the muscle layers?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>It provides a better cosmetic result with a less visible scar</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Better cosmesis is the advantage of the Lanz incision, which uses the same McBurney's point landmark but runs transversely within the skin crease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Each muscle layer is split along its fibre direction, making the wound self-sealing</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The gridiron incision splits each of the three flat muscle layers along their respective fibre directions rather than cutting across them. When tension is released, the fibres close back together, creating a self-sealing wound.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It avoids the inferior epigastric vessels entirely</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Avoiding the inferior epigastric vessels is a general surgical consideration but is not the defining advantage of the gridiron incision over other approaches.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>It allows easy extension if a larger operative field is needed</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Easy extension is an advantage of the midline incision through the linea alba. The gridiron incision, being small and fibre-splitting, is actually difficult to extend.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The gridiron incision's defining advantage is fibre-splitting: each of the three flat muscles is split along its own fibre direction rather than cut. This makes the wound self-sealing and reduces the risk of incisional hernia."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The gridiron incision splits muscles along their fibre directions instead of cutting them, so the wound is self-sealing — the classic advantage for open appendicectomy at McBurney's point."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
